ROCR-Runtime: accept the legacy doorbell types 0 and 1 and restore the
dispatch protocol they need, so that HSA creates an agent for Polaris
(gfx803).

WHY
---
The kernel's kfd_topology.c assigns doorbell type 0 to Kaveri, Hawaii and
Tonga, and type 1 to Carrizo, Fiji, Polaris and Vegam. The GpuAgent
constructor in amd_gpu_agent.cpp accepts only type 2. For any other type it throws
HSA_STATUS_ERROR_INVALID_ISA, and DiscoverGpu then skips the device:

    ROCm/HSA: Skipping unsupported GPU: ...
      Reason: Agent creation failed.
    The GPU node uses unsupported doorbell type 1 (only type 2 is currently supported).

Without this patch, rocminfo lists no gfx803 agent and every HIP program
sees zero devices. AMD closed the request to restore the card as not
planned: https://github.com/ROCm/clr/issues/269.

Accepting the type alone is not enough. AqlQueue::StoreRelaxed rings the
doorbell with type-2 semantics only: one 64-bit store through
signal_.hardware_doorbell_ptr. A legacy doorbell needs the protocol that
upstream deleted in https://github.com/ROCm/rocm-systems/commit/39ecc88. The
runtime keeps a software "max dispatched" watermark under a spinlock, the CP
polls that watermark as its write pointer, and the doorbell is a 32-bit
write. Type 0 writes the ring offset in dwords, and type 1 writes the
dispatch ID. With only the agent check removed, the agent exists, but
dispatch drops packets or writes through a null pointer, because nothing
sets a legacy doorbell pointer.

The protocol code is the fix by an AMD engineer (lucbruni-amd), verified on
an RX 550 with a from-source TheRock build ("rocminfo and clinfo both detect
gfx803 (RX 550) successfully"):
https://github.com/lucbruni-amd/TheRock/commit/3d4ad6093a0069876fbfff590bb56ddb340ae9c5,
file patches/gfx803/rocm-systems/0001-rocr-Restore-legacy-doorbell-support-for-gfx8-Polari.patch.

WHAT
----
- amd_gpu_agent.cpp: the doorbell switch accepts types 0, 1 and 2. Any
  other type still throws HSA_STATUS_ERROR_INVALID_ISA, because StoreRelaxed
  has no protocol for it.
- amd_hsa_signal.h: a 32-bit legacy_hardware_doorbell_ptr next to the
  64-bit hardware_doorbell_ptr in the doorbell union of the signal.
- amd_aql_queue.h and amd_aql_queue.cpp: the queue caches the doorbell type
  of its agent in doorbell_type_. For types 0 and 1, the queue resource
  points the CP write pointer at max_legacy_doorbell_dispatch_id_plus_1, the
  signal kind is AMD_SIGNAL_KIND_LEGACY_DOORBELL, and GetInfo returns
  HSA_STATUS_ERROR_INVALID_QUEUE for HSA_AMD_QUEUE_INFO_DOORBELL_ID, because
  the queue has no 64-bit doorbell. StoreRelaxed gets the legacy branch.

The legacy branch issues the same _mm_sfence() as the type-2 branch before
it publishes the watermark. On gfx8 the AQL ring is mapped write-back, so
x86 store order already puts the packet stores ahead of the doorbell, and
the fence changes nothing there. It keeps both branches correct for a ring
that is mapped write-combining.

aql-ring-queue-full-workaround.patch multiplies the doorbell mask that this
patch restores, so it applies after this patch.

Applies to: rocm-systems release/therock-10.1, commit
7da43026e4f2ef7600433c38c820732f7a205d22.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (gfx803 RX 470, 2026-10-06) against this commit.
Inside an image built from it, rocminfo lists gfx803 as a KERNEL_DISPATCH
agent, and verify.py passes. To check it again, run tools/imgvalidate.sh,
which stops at once when the card is not a dispatch agent.

